      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical lattice clocks combine a powerful advance in precision measurement with broad consequences for fundamental tests, timekeeping, and sensing. Reaching 10^-18 precision is a major experimental achievement.",
        "attribution": "Katori and Ye independently developed leading approaches and demonstrated the field's potential. Their contributions are substantial and complementary, though the wider clock community also played an important role.",
        "maturity": "The techniques have been repeatedly demonstrated and refined over many years; the scientific impact is established.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The stated precision is a milestone rather than a single discovery, so the prize citation would need to define the contribution carefully."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B003",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Christophe Salomon",
        "John E. Thomas",
        "Rudolf Grimm"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Realizing strongly interacting Fermi gases and the BEC-BCS crossover opened a controllable experimental route to fermionic superfluidity and enabled precision studies of strongly correlated many-body physics.",
        "attribution": "These researchers represent major experimental contributions across complementary platforms. The field also rests on broad theoretical and experimental work, so a concise citation would need a clear basis for selecting these three.",
        "maturity": "The crossover and its key properties have been studied in many laboratories over decades, with a strong body of reproducible evidence.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The discovery and realization were distributed across several groups, and the proposed attribution may not capture all decisive contributions."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B016",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Christian J. Bordé",
        "David E. Pritchard",
        "Mark A. Kasevich"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Atom interferometry created exceptionally sensitive instruments for measuring acceleration, rotation, gravity, and fundamental phases. It has opened new ways to test physics and to measure the world.",
        "attribution": "Bordé, Pritchard, and Kasevich are prominent pioneers of atom interferometry and its development as a precision tool. The contribution spans distinct early methods and later high-sensitivity demonstrations.",
        "maturity": "The method has been validated across independent laboratories and has progressed from foundational demonstrations to sophisticated sensors.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The proposed discovery covers a broad development history, making the boundaries of the prize contribution and the selection of laureates uncertain."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B008",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Alexei Kitaev",
        "Andrew M. Steane",
        "Peter W. Shor"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Quantum error correction and fault tolerance showed how coherent quantum information can in principle be protected against errors, turning quantum computation from a fragile ideal into a physically meaningful program.",
        "attribution": "Kitaev, Steane, and Shor made landmark contributions to topological codes, error-correcting codes, and fault-tolerant quantum computation. The central ideas arose through distinct, complementary work.",
        "maturity": "The theory is mature and its importance is clear; experimental implementations have made significant progress but remain an evolving area.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The separation between foundational theory and large-scale practical fault tolerance remains unsettled."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B020",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Juan Ignacio Cirac",
        "Peter Zoller",
        "Rainer Blatt"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Trapped ions provided a remarkably controlled route to quantum logic and simulation, linking precise manipulation of individual atoms with the possibility of scalable quantum information processing.",
        "attribution": "Cirac and Zoller laid important theoretical foundations, while Blatt led decisive experimental demonstrations. This theory-experiment connection makes the attribution compelling, although the field has many major contributors.",
        "maturity": "The basic operations and small quantum processors have been demonstrated repeatedly. The long-term scale and practical reach of the platform are still developing.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The proposed discovery joins foundational proposals with later experimental realization, and the appropriate scope of a citation is not self-evident."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B006",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Immanuel Bloch",
        "Markus Greiner",
        "Tilman Esslinger"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Ultracold atoms in optical lattices made many-body quantum systems unusually clean and controllable, enabling direct study of strongly correlated matter and quantum simulation.",
        "attribution": "Bloch, Greiner, and Esslinger made influential experimental advances across lattice loading, many-body phases, and quantum simulation. Theoretical proposals, including work by Cirac and Zoller, are also central to the field's development.",
        "maturity": "The platform is established and has produced a rich experimental record, though the breadth of phenomena studied makes a single prize-sized contribution harder to isolate.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The discovery combines a general experimental platform with many subsequent results, and credit is spread across theory and multiple laboratories."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B038",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Peter F. Moulton",
        "Ursula Keller",
        "Wilson Sibbett"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The titanium-sapphire laser and practical passive mode-locking helped make femtosecond pulses broadly available, transforming ultrafast spectroscopy and many areas of experimental science.",
        "attribution": "Moulton's development of the Ti:sapphire laser and Keller's and Sibbett's contributions to practical mode-locking represent complementary advances. The wider laser community also contributed to the mature technology.",
        "maturity": "The technology has been in sustained use for decades, with clear and durable impact across physics and beyond.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The framing combines a laser medium with multiple mode-locking techniques, so the most coherent discovery and division of credit would need careful definition."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B024",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Fedor Jelezko",
        "Jörg Wrachtrup",
        "Mikhail D. Lukin"
